This is a hexspace adapter for Sequel, designed to be used with Spark (not
Hive). You can use the hexspace:// protocol in the Sequel connection URL
to use this adapter.

== Emulated DELETE and UPDATE

Spark has no DELETE and no UPDATE, so Sequel::Dataset#delete and
Sequel::Dataset#update are emulated. Both run as:

1. CREATE TABLE <table>__sequel_delete_emulate AS SELECT <the rows the
   operation should leave behind>
2. INSERT OVERWRITE TABLE <table> SELECT ... FROM
   <table>__sequel_delete_emulate
3. check that <table> now holds as many rows as the temp table did, then DROP
   the temp table

This is not a transaction, and this adapter has none to offer you:
Database#transaction just yields. The adapter's own source says this path is
"designed to minimize the changes to the tests, and is not recommended for
production use". The rest of this section is what that disclaimer means in
practice, so that you can decide rather than discover.

=== What is guaranteed

* Your table is never dropped. There is no window in which reading it fails
  with table-or-view-not-found. (Before 2026-10-02 the swap dropped your table
  and renamed the temp table into its place, and a process that died in between
  left the table GONE.)

* The rows the operation intends to keep exist under
  <table>__sequel_delete_emulate from step 1 until step 3. If the process dies
  before step 3, or if the row-count check in step 3 disagrees, that table is
  left in place on purpose -- it is the recovery copy, and it is the only name
  your data may be sitting under. Nothing in your own source will name it, so
  if an emulated delete or update died, look there first.

* Step 3 does not drop the temp table on the strength of step 2 merely
  returning. Until the row count has been checked, the temp table is treated as
  the only intact copy.

=== What is NOT guaranteed

* Atomicity. INSERT OVERWRITE is unambiguously atomic only on an ACID table
  such as Delta. This adapter emits USING <format> only when you pass :using to
  create_table, and the temp table above passes none, so unless your metastore
  defaults to an ACID format both tables are not ACID. Against a default Spark
  thriftserver (Provider hive, TextInputFormat, LazySimpleSerDe) the write was
  measured to stage and then commit -- a job that failed after writing 150,000
  of 200,000 rows left the table holding its full pre-operation contents, and
  SIGKILLing the client at seven points across the statement never left the
  table absent, empty or partial. What is left is the commit step: replacing a
  multi-file Hive table in place is not one atomic filesystem operation, so a
  death of the SERVER, or a storage failure, inside that step can leave the
  table PRESENT but empty or partial. A plain SELECT then returns the wrong
  rows SILENTLY rather than failing loudly. That window has not been
  reproduced and is not quantified; it is accepted, not closed.

* Re-running is not free. Step 1 is preceded by a DROP TABLE IF EXISTS of
  <table>__sequel_delete_emulate, because Spark's CREATE TABLE is not
  idempotent and a stranded temp table otherwise makes every later delete or
  update on the same table fail. That means re-running a delete or update that
  died DESTROYS the recovery copy. Recover your data first, then re-run.

* Concurrency. The temp table name is derived from the target table name, so
  two emulated deletes or updates against the same table at the same time will
  fight over it. Serialize them.

If any of that is unacceptable for your workload, do not use emulated DELETE or
UPDATE -- build the replacement table yourself, under a name you control, and
swap it in on terms you have chosen.
